From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Junod <donujp@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 13:49:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] feat(session): add an optional session-wide cap on live peers

peer_limit bounds the live peers of one torrent, but each torrent owns
its own semaphore, so the number of peer tasks a session can have grows
linearly with the number of torrents. An application that manages many
torrents has no way to put a ceiling on the total.

Add SessionOptions::peer_limit_total (Option<usize>, default None). When
set, one semaphore is shared by every torrent in the session. A live
peer now holds a PeerPermit that owns both its per-torrent permit and,
if configured, a session permit, until the peer manager exits:

 - incoming peers try both limits without waiting and are dropped, as
   before, if either is full;
 - outgoing peers wait for both before spawning their manager. The
   per-torrent permit is taken first, and the single peer-adder task of
   a torrent holds at most one permit while it waits for a session slot.

With None the behaviour is unchanged, including the 128 per-torrent
fallback. The option is read at session construction; there is no live
resizing, and it does not bound handshake work before an incoming
connection is routed to a torrent.

The tests cover the per-torrent boundary, two torrents sharing one
session pool (the fourth peer is rejected and admitted again after a
release), and a waiting outgoing acquisition resuming once the shared
permit is dropped. The test module sits at the end of the file.
